            "id": "https://mariia-osipova.github.io/my-page/cheese-agent/",
            "url": "https://mariia-osipova.github.io/my-page/cheese-agent/",
            "title": "Cheese Agent: A FastAPI Chatbot with Vector Search",
            "summary": "AI-powered cheese agent built with FastAPI, AstraDB vector search, OpenAI, and a lightweight JS frontend.",
            "content_html": "<p>This post is about my experience building <a href=\"https://github.com/mariia-osipova/cheese-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Cheese Agent</a>, a cheese-themed chatbot project with a FastAPI backend, OpenAI embeddings, AstraDB vector search, and a vanilla JS frontend.</p><h2>What this post is</h2><p>This is a short technical reflection on building Cheese Agent, especially the parts that made it feel like a real systems project rather than just a UI experiment.</p><h2>Why I made it</h2><p>I wanted to build something that combined an interface people would actually enjoy using with a backend architecture that forced me to think about retrieval quality, data organization, and deployment.</p><h2>How it works</h2><p>The backend exposes a chat endpoint in FastAPI. When a user sends a question, the backend embeds that query with OpenAI, searches AstraDB, and sends the retrieved context into the generation step.</p><h2>What I focused on in the implementation</h2><p>The most useful parts for me were the ingestion script, the vector database, and the split between a simple frontend and a backend that handled retrieval and answer generation.</p><h2>What seems promising so far</h2><p>This kind of project is useful because it combines data preparation, embeddings, retrieval, API design, frontend integration, and deployment in one place.</p><h2>Closing thoughts</h2><p>For me, Cheese Agent became a compact full-stack AI project where the model, the database, the data pipeline, and the interface all had to line up.</p>",
            "image": "/my-page/media/posts/5/Group-42-3.png",
